# Visual Feedback Loop

Use this skill when the task depends on how a web UI looks, not just whether the code compiles. Examples: matching a screenshot, cloning a landing page, reverse engineering a design, polishing a UI, comparing local output to a reference, or using Playwright to inspect and iterate.

## Operating Rules

- Always capture screenshots before making visual claims.
- Use Playwright MCP when available for quick interactive inspection: accessibility snapshots for structure, screenshots for visual verification, resize for responsive checks, console messages for debugging.
- If MCP tools are not available, use local Playwright scripts or the project's existing Playwright setup.
- Check desktop, tablet, and mobile unless the user gives specific dimensions.
- Fix the largest visible mismatches first: layout, hero composition, type scale, spacing, colors, images, shadows, borders, radius, then micro details.
- After each pass, capture fresh screenshots. Do not assume a CSS change worked.
- Keep changes targeted. Prefer layout/CSS/Tailwind adjustments over component rewrites unless the structure prevents matching.
- Do not fake completion. Only say it matches after screenshots were captured and inspected.

## Default Viewports

Use these unless the user specifies otherwise:

```ts
const viewports = [
  { name: "desktop", width: 1440, height: 900 },
  { name: "tablet", width: 1024, height: 768 },
  { name: "mobile", width: 390, height: 844 },
];
```

## Workflow

1. Identify the reference: uploaded screenshot, live URL, local image, competitor page, or specific section.
2. Start or locate the local app using the existing project command from `package.json` or local docs.
3. Capture the reference and local implementation at the same viewport sizes.
4. Inspect screenshots and, when possible, generate pixel diffs.
5. List the top 3-7 mismatches before editing.
6. Apply a focused pass of UI changes.
7. Re-capture and compare.
8. Repeat up to 5 passes unless the user sets a different budget.

Default stop criteria:

```text
Desktop diff < 5%
Tablet diff < 7%
Mobile diff < 8%
```

Treat these as guidelines. Pixel diffs can overstate harmless differences from images, fonts, antialiasing, icons, and browser rendering.

## Playwright MCP

When Playwright MCP is configured, prefer it for interactive inspection before writing scripts:

- `browser_navigate`: open reference or local URLs.
- `browser_snapshot`: inspect the accessibility tree and locate elements by role/text.
- `browser_take_screenshot`: capture the page or element for visual verification.
- `browser_resize`: test responsive layouts.
- `browser_click`, `browser_hover`, `browser_wait_for`: reach interactive states and dismiss banners/popups.
- `browser_console_messages`: check runtime errors while debugging visual issues.
- `browser_install`: install the configured browser if needed.

Official setup pattern:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "playwright": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["@playwright/mcp@latest"]
    }
  }
}
```

For headless runs:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "playwright": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["@playwright/mcp@latest", "--headless"]
    }
  }
}
```

MCP snapshots are structural, not pixel analysis. Use them to understand page state and interact reliably; use screenshots and diffs to judge visual fidelity.

## Screenshot Capture

Before screenshots:

- Wait for network idle when practical.
- Wait for fonts to load.
- Disable animations and smooth scrolling.
- Hide cookie banners, modals, chat widgets, and other blockers if they are not part of the target.
- Use the same viewport and device scale factor for reference and actual.

Create artifacts under:

```text
.visual-feedback/
  reference-desktop.png
  actual-desktop-pass-1.png
  diff-desktop-pass-1.png
  notes-pass-1.md
```

Minimal capture script:

```ts
import { chromium } from "playwright";
import fs from "fs";
import path from "path";

const outputDir = ".visual-feedback";
const viewports = [
  { name: "desktop", width: 1440, height: 900 },
  { name: "tablet", width: 1024, height: 768 },
  { name: "mobile", width: 390, height: 844 },
];

async function capture(url: string, label: string) {
  fs.mkdirSync(outputDir, { recursive: true });
  const browser = await chromium.launch();

  for (const viewport of viewports) {
    const page = await browser.newPage({
      viewport: { width: viewport.width, height: viewport.height },
      deviceScaleFactor: 1,
    });

    await page.goto(url, { waitUntil: "networkidle" });
    await page.evaluate(async () => {
      await document.fonts.ready;
      const style = document.createElement("style");
      style.textContent = `
        *, *::before, *::after {
          animation-duration: 0s !important;
          animation-delay: 0s !important;
          transition-duration: 0s !important;
          transition-delay: 0s !important;
          scroll-behavior: auto !important;
        }
      `;
      document.head.appendChild(style);
    });

    await page.screenshot({
      path: path.join(outputDir, `${label}-${viewport.name}.png`),
      fullPage: true,
    });
    await page.close();
  }

  await browser.close();
}

const [url, label] = process.argv.slice(2);
if (!url || !label) {
  console.error("Usage: tsx capture.ts <url> <label>");
  process.exit(1);
}

capture(url, label);
```

## Visual Diff

Use `pixelmatch` and `pngjs` when the project does not already have a visual comparison helper:

```bash
npm install -D pixelmatch pngjs playwright
```

Minimal diff script:

```ts
import fs from "fs";
import path from "path";
import pixelmatch from "pixelmatch";
import { PNG } from "pngjs";

const outputDir = ".visual-feedback";
const viewports = ["desktop", "tablet", "mobile"];
const results = [];

function compare(referencePath: string, actualPath: string, diffPath: string) {
  const reference = PNG.sync.read(fs.readFileSync(referencePath));
  const actual = PNG.sync.read(fs.readFileSync(actualPath));
  const width = Math.min(reference.width, actual.width);
  const height = Math.min(reference.height, actual.height);
  const refCropped = new PNG({ width, height });
  const actualCropped = new PNG({ width, height });
  const diff = new PNG({ width, height });

  PNG.bitblt(reference, refCropped, 0, 0, width, height, 0, 0);
  PNG.bitblt(actual, actualCropped, 0, 0, width, height, 0, 0);

  const diffPixels = pixelmatch(
    refCropped.data,
    actualCropped.data,
    diff.data,
    width,
    height,
    { threshold: 0.1 }
  );

  fs.writeFileSync(diffPath, PNG.sync.write(diff));
  const totalPixels = width * height;
  return {
    width,
    height,
    diffPixels,
    totalPixels,
    diffPercent: Number(((diffPixels / totalPixels) * 100).toFixed(2)),
  };
}

for (const viewport of viewports) {
  const referencePath = path.join(outputDir, `reference-${viewport}.png`);
  const actualPath = path.join(outputDir, `actual-${viewport}.png`);
  const diffPath = path.join(outputDir, `diff-${viewport}.png`);
  if (fs.existsSync(referencePath) && fs.existsSync(actualPath)) {
    results.push({ viewport, ...compare(referencePath, actualPath, diffPath) });
  }
}

fs.writeFileSync(
  path.join(outputDir, "diff-results.json"),
  JSON.stringify(results, null, 2)
);
console.table(results);
```

## Inspection Checklist

Layout:

- Page width, max-width, grid/column alignment, section order, hero composition, fold composition.

Typography:

- Font family, size, weight, line height, letter spacing, text color, heading width, paragraph width.

Spacing:

- Section padding, nav-to-hero gap, heading-to-copy gap, CTA gap, card padding, grid gaps, image margins.

Colors:

- Backgrounds, text, muted text, accent color, buttons, borders, gradients, overlays.

Components:

- Button height/radius/shadow, card radius/border/shadow, input height, badge style, navbar height.

Images:

- Asset choice, crop, aspect ratio, object-fit, position, size, shadow/glow.

Responsive:

- Mobile stacking, nav behavior, hero image visibility, font scaling, CTA wrapping, horizontal overflow.

## Tailwind Matching

For pixel-specific references, exact arbitrary values are acceptable:

```tsx
className="text-[64px] leading-[0.95] tracking-[-0.04em]"
className="mx-auto max-w-[1180px] px-[32px] py-[96px]"
className="h-[48px] rounded-[14px] px-[22px] text-[15px] font-semibold"
```

Do not overuse broad classes like `text-5xl`, `py-20`, or `gap-8` when the reference is visibly pixel-specific.

## Reference Page Analysis

For live references, inspect CSS and loaded assets when useful:

```ts
const data = await page.evaluate(() => ({
  title: document.title,
  bodyText: document.body.innerText.slice(0, 3000),
  fonts: [...document.fonts].map((font) => ({
    family: font.family,
    weight: font.weight,
    style: font.style,
    status: font.status,
  })),
  stylesheets: [...document.querySelectorAll("link")]
    .map((link) => ({ rel: link.rel, href: link.href }))
    .filter((x) => x.rel.includes("stylesheet") || x.href.includes("font")),
}));
```

For colors and type samples:

```ts
const samples = await page.evaluate(() =>
  [...document.querySelectorAll("*")].slice(0, 200).map((el) => {
    const style = getComputedStyle(el);
    return {
      tag: el.tagName,
      className: String(el.className),
      color: style.color,
      backgroundColor: style.backgroundColor,
      borderColor: style.borderColor,
      fontFamily: style.fontFamily,
      fontSize: style.fontSize,
      fontWeight: style.fontWeight,
      lineHeight: style.lineHeight,
    };
  })
);
```

## Pass Notes

Write a short notes file after each pass:

```md
# Visual Feedback Pass 1

## Diff Summary

- Desktop: 14.2%
- Tablet: 18.5%
- Mobile: 21.7%

## Biggest Mismatches

1. Hero heading is too small by approximately 12px.
2. Hero section starts too low; reduce top padding by around 32px.
3. CTA buttons are too rounded and too tall.

## Changes Applied

- Increased hero heading to `text-[68px]`.
- Reduced hero top padding from `pt-32` to `pt-20`.
- Changed button radius from `rounded-full` to `rounded-[14px]`.

## Remaining Issues

- Mobile hero image still stacks too low.
- Card shadows are too strong.
```

